            <ArticleTitle>Radiation exposure to patient's skin during percutaneous coronary intervention for various lesions, including chronic total occlusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>44-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Radiation skin injuries have been reported as a result of various procedures, so in the present study the patients' entrance skin dose (ESD) during percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) was evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">ESDs were assessed during 97 procedures (13 for chronic total occlusion (CTO), 14 for multivessel stenoses, 22 for single-vessel multiple stenoses, and 48 for single stenosis). The patients wore jackets that had 48 or 52 radiosensitive indicators placed on the back during the PCI procedures, with 8 other indicators placed on both upper arms. After the procedure, the color of the indicators was analyzed with a color measuring instrument, and the patients' ESDs were calculated from the color difference of the indicators. The average maximum ESDs of the patients were 4.5 +/- 2.8 Gy (median: 4.6 Gy) for CTO, 2.3 +/- 0.7 Gy (median: 2.4 Gy) for multivessel stenoses, 1.8 +/- 1.0 Gy (median: 1.5 Gy) for single-vessel multiple stenoses, and 1.4 +/- 0.9 Gy (median: 1.2 Gy) for single stenosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Skin injury can occur during PCI, especially for CTO, so it is important to estimate each patient's ESD and attempt to reduce it.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
